{"id":"9f1d1fcd-861f-45fa-89e3-f8aa1bc7f0ea","object":"chat.completion","created":1784825306,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import json\\n\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must be a dict\\\")\\n    if 'nodes' not in case or 'required_symbols' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"missing required keys: nodes, required_symbols\\\")\\n\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"nodes and required_symbols must be lists\\\")\\n\\n    # build maps\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid in node_map:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"duplicate node id: {nid}\\\")\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        children_map.setdefault(node['parent'], []).append(nid)\\n\\n    # find root\\n    roots = [nid for nid, nd in node_map.items() if nd['parent'] is None]\\n    if len(roots) != 1:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"must have exactly one root (parent None)\\\")\\n    root_id = roots[0]\\n\\n    # validate root symbol\\n    if node_map[root_id]['symbol'] != 'root':\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"root symbol must be 'root'\\\")\\n\\n    # initial retained set and symbol counts\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for nd in node_map.values():\\n        sym = nd['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n\\n    # validate required symbols exist\\n    required_set = set(required_symbols)\\n    for sym in required_set:\\n        if symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) == 0:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"required symbol '{sym}' not present in any node\\\")\\n\\n    # build candidate list: removable, not required symbol, not root\\n    candidates = []\\n    for nid, nd in node_map.items():\\n        if nd['removable'] and nd['symbol'] not in required_set and nid != root_id:\\n            candidates.append((nd['depth'], nid))\\n\\n    # sort: deeper first, then ID ascending\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))\\n\\n    removal_order = []\\n\\n    def remove_subtree(nid):\\n        stack = [nid]\\n        while stack:\\n            cur = stack.pop()\\n            if cur in retained:\\n                retained.remove(cur)\\n                sym = node_map[cur]['symbol']\\n                symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n                for child in children_map.get(cur, []):\\n                    if child in retained:\\n                        stack.append(child)\\n\\n    for depth, nid in candidates:\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        # check children retained\\n        has_retained_child = any(child in retained for child in children_map.get(nid, []))\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # compute subtree symbols to be removed\\n        # we do a quick walk to get counts\\n        subtree_counts = {}\\n        stack = [nid]\\n        while stack:\\n            cur = stack.pop()\\n            if cur in retained:\\n                sym = node_map[cur]['symbol']\\n                subtree_counts[sym] = subtree_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n                for child in children_map.get(cur, []):\\n                    if child in retained:\\n                        stack.append(child)\\n\\n        # check required symbols survive\\n        feasible = True\\n        for sym in required_set:\\n            if symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) - subtree_counts.get(sym, 0) < 1:\\n                feasible = False\\n                break\\n\\n        if not feasible:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # remove\\n        remove_subtree(nid)\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n\\n    result = {\\n        \\\"retained\\\": sorted(retained),\\n        \\\"removal_order\\\": removal_order\\n    }\\n    return result\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a Python function `solve(case)` that implements grammar-guided node reduction. The problem is about reducing a parse tree while preserving required symbols and an interestingness oracle. The input case has nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. There are required_symbols. The goal: given a tree, we want to remove removable nodes (and possibly their subtrees) in a grammar-valid way, but we need to preserve required symbols and the root. Also we need to consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes, and for ties use node ID. The output should be the set of retained node IDs (presumably after reduction) and the order of removals.\n\nBut careful: The problem statement: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" - we need to reduce the tree by deleting nodes that are removable, but we cannot delete nodes that are required or have children that are retained (i.e., we can only delete leaves? Actually in parse tree reduction, you can delete a node only if all its children are also deleted? Or you can replace a subtree with a leaf? The description: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" And \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So a node can be removed only if it has no retained children (i.e., all its descendants that are retained are already removed? Actually \"node with retained children\" means if any child of that node is retained, we cannot remove the node. So we can only remove nodes that are leaves in the current retained set. And also we need to check that after removal, all required symbols are still present somewhere in the tree.\n\nThe order: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we process nodes in decreasing depth (deepest first). For same depth, tie-break by node ID (lexicographically? Probably string comparison, but we need stable). The removal order should be recorded in the order they are actually removed.\n\nAlso we need to use only quantities in the payload. No randomness.\n\nThe output: Return \"sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we need to return something like {\"retained\": [...], \"removal_order\": [...]}. The retained IDs should be sorted? It says \"sorted retained IDs\" so sort them (probably ascending lexicographically). The removal order is the order in which removals happened.\n\nAlso we need to handle case where no removable nodes? Possibly remove nothing.\n\nWe must raise ValueError for infeasible cases.\n\nNow, the function signature: def solve(case): case is a dict with keys: nodes (list of dicts), required_symbols (list), _case_nonce (optional). We need to validate fields.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Parse case. Ensure required fields exist. Could raise ValueError if not.\n\n2. Build a map from node id to node dict. Also build children map: for each node, list of child ids. Also track parent.\n\n3. The root is the node with parent None. Ensure there is exactly one root. The root's symbol should be 'root'. Also root is always retained.\n\n4. Identify required symbols set from required_symbols list. Root symbol is required by definition? The list includes 'root'. So we need to ensure that after removals, every symbol in required_symbols appears in the retained nodes' symbols. We don't need to keep all occurrences, just at least one node with that symbol.\n\n5. Initially, all nodes are considered retained. We'll maintain a set of retained node IDs.\n\n6. Determine removable nodes: those with removable=True. But we cannot remove root, nor nodes with retained children.\n\n7. Process nodes in order: sort all removable nodes by depth descending, then by node ID ascending (string comparison). We'll iterate over this list and attempt to remove each node if conditions are met. But note: after removing a node, the retained set changes, and other nodes may become removable (their children may be gone). The order we process should be based on initial depth? The instruction: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That suggests we should process in that order, but also after removals, we need to reconsider nodes that may now have no retained children? However, the typical Perses algorithm processes nodes in a specific order and checks removability. The problem states: \"registered_candidate_order\" as a hard contract. I think we need to generate a list of candidate nodes in the order (depth descending, id ascending) and then for each candidate, decide whether to remove it. And if we remove it, we update retained set, and continue. But we should not re-sort after removals because the order is predetermined. However, instructions say \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That is a fixed order for evaluating candidates. So we sort all removable nodes initially by depth descending, then ID ascending. Then iterate through that list. For each node, check if it is still in retained set, and if its children are all not retained (i.e., none of its children are in retained set), and if removing it (and its subtree) would still leave all required symbols present in the retained set. If all conditions hold, we remove it: remove from retained set, record its id in removal_order list. (Note: removing a node implies removing its entire subtree? But the node may have descendants that are already removed? Actually if we remove a node, we remove that node and all its descendants from retained set. But if some descendants were already removed, it's fine. However, we must ensure we don't remove a node if any of its children are retained. That condition ensures we don't remove a node that still has retained children. So we only remove leaf nodes (in the current retained tree). When we remove a node, we effectively prune that whole branch. This is typical of Perses reduction: you can replace a subtree with a leaf (or delete it). But here we are only allowed to delete nodes if all their children are already deleted (or never existed). So it's a bottom-up deletion. So the approach is correct.\n\nImportant: The condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" after removal: we need to check that for each required symbol, there exists at least one retained node (excluding the node we are about to remove) that has that symbol. Since we are only removing one node (and its subtree), we can check by counting occurrences of each required symbol in the current retained set, and ensure that for each required symbol, count after removal >= 1. But careful: the node we remove might be the only node with that symbol. So we need to subtract its symbol from the count.\n\nWe can precompute a dictionary of symbol counts in initially retained set. Then as we remove nodes, we decrement counts. But we need to handle that removing a node removes its symbol and also the symbols of all descendants (since subtree is removed). So we need to recursively compute the symbols of the subtree of the node being removed. When we remove a node, we should remove all its descendants. But note: if we have already removed some descendants earlier (since we process deepest first), then those descendants are no longer in retained set. So when removing a node, we only need to remove the node itself and any descendants that are still retained. But since we process deepest first, we will have removed deeper nodes before shallower nodes. So when we get to a shallow node, its descendants might already be gone. So we need to know which descendants are still retained. But we can compute the subtree of a node (all descendants) and then intersect with retained set to remove. However, it's simpler: when we remove a node, we can recursively remove all nodes in its subtree that are still in the retained set. We can do a depth-first from the node, removing all found. And update symbol counts accordingly.\n\nBut the order of processing: we process nodes in decreasing depth. So a node at depth 2 is processed before its parent at depth 1. That means when we consider the parent, its children might already be removed. So the parent will have no retained children, and then we can consider removing it if conditions hold. That's correct.\n\nNow, about \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" Our check ensures that. Also \"Never remove the root\" - root is not removable (removable=False) so it won't be in candidate list.\n\nAlso \"Never remove a required symbol\" - required symbol means a node whose symbol is in required_symbols? The problem says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It says \"a required symbol\" meaning any node that has a symbol that is in required_symbols? Or does it mean the symbol itself? I think it means we cannot remove any node whose symbol is listed as required. But the root symbol is already required. Also in the example, nodes with symbol 's1' are required (required_symbols includes \"s1\"). So we must not remove any node that has a symbol in required_symbols. However, some nodes with required symbol might have removable=True. So we need to skip those nodes from removal candidates. So when building the candidate list, we should only include nodes that are removable AND whose symbol is NOT in required_symbols. Also, after removal, we need to ensure that the required symbol still appears somewhere else. So we cannot remove the only node with that required symbol. That's covered by the symbol count check.\n\nThus, the candidate list: nodes where removable=True and node.symbol not in required_symbols set (including root? root symbol is 'root' which is in required_symbols, so root is excluded anyway). But note: the required_symbols list includes 'root' and 's1'. So we cannot remove any node with symbol 's1' even if it is removable? In the examples, n4 has symbol 's1' and removable=True. But since 's1' is required, we cannot remove n4. So n4